                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Trackless is a strange blend of first person exploration and text-based puzzles on a wonderfully mysterious sci-fi world. It's structured around the pilgrimage to see a sacred \"object\", and most of the game is about getting through various obstructions and trials on the way. Exploration is richly rewarded with snippets of information, some optional challenges and a score system that can be used to buy different skins for your phone (which sometimes give it new, unexplained abilities).\n\n As you wander around, you can explore at your leisure and interact with objects in the environment. Most of them will give you some flavor text, but a few are marked as interactive; if you right click on them, a text box appears. So, for example you might find a machete next to some vines, and type \"hack\" into the textbox that appears when you interact with the machete. All puzzles are self-contained like that, though there are several that chain actions as you complete them. The game also rewards you with more score if you don't reuse words, and if you use a word that's not just functional but one that works better in context; for example, on that machete puzzle you could get away with just typing \"cut\", but it would give you fewer points. It's a pretty unique mechanic that's pretty satisfying when it works. As with any text-based game, it has some \"guess the verb\" issues, but since figuring out the intention of the interaction is part of the fun it doesn't grate too much. Its limited vocabulary is a bit more of a problem, since quite a few entries I tried didn't work (at one point throwing me off-track for a puzzle).\n In any case, once you get into the spirit of things there's nothing too hard in there, which keeps things pacey. It also helps that the phone you carry around in front of you all the time points you in the direction of any objects in the vicinity, both interactive and not.\n\n The world is beautifully realized with a wonderful soundtrack and a great, euro-comic style aesthetic, making exploration a joy. The writing does a great job of explaining just enough to keep you interested, with lots of little details building up to a very well realized sense of place.\n\n There's nothing else out there quite like Trackless, and I enjoyed my time with it thoroughly.",
